/**
 * @page vfs_architecture VFS Architecture
 *
 * @section vfs_arch_object_model Object model
 *
 * The VFS object model separates file-system state from the path state of an
 * execution context. The class hierarchy is:
 * - @p vfs_fs_c, the abstract file system. Its methods, stat, open
 *   directory, open file, unlink, rename, mkdir and rmdir, take absolute
 *   paths.
 * - @p vfs_overlay_driver_c, derived from @p vfs_fs_c. It registers other
 *   file systems under names and delegates the other paths to an optional
 *   overlaid file system.
 * - @p vfs_root_c, derived from @p vfs_overlay_driver_c. It adds the current
 *   directory, accepts relative paths and can add a path prefix to the
 *   paths delegated to its overlaid file system.
 * - @p vfs_node_c, the abstract node, with @p vfs_directory_node_c and
 *   @p vfs_file_node_c derived from it. Each node refers to the file system
 *   that owns it.
 * .
 * File-system implementations, including non-root overlays, derive from
 * @p vfs_fs_c. A @p vfs_root_c is an overlay specialized as the entry point
 * of an application or sandbox.
 *
 * The process-aware root and global path API are enabled by
 * @p VFS_CFG_ENABLE_DRV_ROOT. Root support requires the overlay driver, but
 * the core VFS API, concrete file systems and non-root overlays can be used
 * with root support disabled.
 *
 * @section vfs_arch_buffers Shared buffer pairs
 *
 * Each shared pool element reserves two full path buffers atomically.
 * @p paths.path1 and @p paths.path2 each hold @p VFS_CFG_PATHLEN_MAX characters
 * plus the terminator; @p buf is a single scratch view of the same storage.
 * @p VFS_BUFFER_SIZE is the combined scratch capacity. The element also has
 * pointer alignment and may contain trailing alignment padding, which is not
 * part of that capacity. @p VFS_CFG_PATHBUFS_NUM counts pairs.
 *
 * @p vfs_buffer_take_wait() waits on the guarded pool without holding an
 * upper-layer VFS lock. It returns a whole pair, released with
 * @p vfs_buffer_release(). A caller retaining a pair must not wait for a
 * second pair from the same pool, including through delegated calls or
 * callbacks. Do not hold a lock needed by a thread returning a pair.
 *
 * Each root path operation allocates, uses, and returns its pair locally.
 * It reads CWD and routing state after allocation. Overlay and ROMFS consume
 * borrowed read-only paths without reserving another pair. A driver may
 * retain these paths throughout its call, including waits. Borrowed paths
 * expire on return.
 * Root is an outer entry point: do not stack a root below another root or
 * reenter an allocating root operation while retaining a pair.
 *
 * @section vfs_arch_lifetime Object lifetime
 *
 * File-system objects derive from @p base_object_c and are not reference
 * counted. Their lifetime is managed by the caller. Overlays and roots borrow
 * their backing and registered file systems. Unregistering a file system only
 * removes its mapping, and disposing an overlay or root does not dispose any
 * backing or registered file systems. The same file system can therefore be
 * shared by multiple overlays or roots.
 *
 * Nodes are reference counted, but their owner pointers do not retain a
 * reference to the file system. Each file system, including an overlay or
 * root, must remain alive until all its nodes have been released and all its
 * operations have finished. Before disposing a file system, the caller must
 * also remove every mapping or backing relationship that could access it.
 * Unregistering a mapping does not close nodes previously opened through it
 * and does not wait for the operations already routed through it.
 * Mounting, unmounting or formatting a native file system, and reconfiguring
 * a backend, require that no operation is active on it and that none of its
 * nodes is open.
 *
 * @section vfs_arch_fs_paths File-system path contract
 *
 * Except for root entry points described below, every path passed to a
 * @p vfs_fs_c virtual method shall be:
 *
 * - absolute, beginning with a slash;
 * - normalized, with no repeated separators;
 * - free of @c "." and @c ".." elements;
 * - free of a trailing separator, except for the root path @c "/".
 *
 * Non-root file-system methods do not consult or modify a current directory.
 * Mounted file systems and nodes refer to @p vfs_fs_c objects, so the same
 * file system can be shared by multiple roots.
 *
 * @section vfs_arch_root_paths Root path contract
 *
 * Root methods and the top-level path API accept both absolute and relative
 * paths. A root resolves relative inputs against its own current directory
 * and normalizes all input paths before calling the selected file system.
 * The top-level API uses the default root. A rename resolves both paths from
 * the same current-directory snapshot. Changing the current directory
 * preserves its logical candidate through validation and node cleanup before
 * committing it.
 * @p vfsRootOpen() keeps one resolved logical path across file-to-directory
 * fallback even if CWD changes while a driver sleeps. Use it for combined
 * opens through a root; @p vfsFSOpen() borrows a normalized absolute path.
 *
 * Root owns the optional backing prefix. Registered mounts bypass it; only
 * backing paths receive the prefix. Opening logical @c "/" merges registered
 * names with the backing directory at the prefix. CWD stays in the logical
 * namespace and never includes that prefix. The prefix is an argument of
 * @p vfsrootObjectInit(), @p ovldrvObjectInit() only takes the backing file
 * system. The prefix must remain valid for the lifetime of the root.
 *
 * Root path state is private to the root instance. Two roots may therefore
 * share mounted file systems without sharing their current directories.
 * Directory nodes remain per-open handles and are not file-system objects.
 *
 * @section vfs_arch_concurrency Concurrency contract
 *
 * The VFS does not serialize operations. Path working storage is not part of
 * the objects: each root operation takes a buffer pair from the shared pool
 * for its whole duration, see @ref vfs_arch_buffers, and overlays route the
 * paths they receive without modifying their own state. Concurrent
 * operations, through the same root or through roots sharing an overlay,
 * therefore never share path storage.
 *
 * The mount table of an overlay or root and the current directory of a root
 * are protected by an optional mutex in the overlay object, inherited by the
 * root, enabled by @p VFS_CFG_USE_MUTUAL_EXCLUSION:
 * - the mutex covers mount lookups and updates and the copy or commit of the
 *   current directory, it is released before calling another file system, a
 *   node or a callback and before any blocking allocation, so operations
 *   through the same root are not serialized;
 * - a rename selects both routes in one step at each layer, and a root
 *   resolves both paths against the same current directory;
 * - a directory change is validated without the mutex and committed after,
 *   concurrent successful changes take effect in commit order. The current
 *   directory buffer is allocated with @p chCoreAlloc() on the first
 *   successful change and never released;
 * - with the option disabled, the default, there is no mutex and changing
 *   the current directory of a root, or registering and unregistering file
 *   systems, must not run concurrently with other operations on the same
 *   object.
 * .
 * Enumerating the top directory of an overlay or root is a live view of its
 * mount table: mount changes during the enumeration can skip or repeat
 * mounted entries, a new enumeration starts from the current table.
 *
 * Nodes are not protected by the VFS:
 * - a reference keeps a node alive, it does not serialize its operations;
 * - reference counters are protected by the OOP layer, concurrent use needs
 *   a synchronized OOP configuration, @p OOP_USE_NOTHING does not provide
 *   it;
 * - operations on the same open node, including duplicated descriptors
 *   sharing it, are serialized by the callers unless its driver states
 *   otherwise, multi-call sequences need caller ordering too;
 * - distinct nodes can be used concurrently only as far as the leaf file
 *   system and its backend allow.
 * .
 * Direct driver methods and the convenience API follow the same rules, the
 * convenience functions check their pointer arguments without reading the
 * reference counters.
 * @subsection vfs_arch_leaf_locking Leaf file systems
 *
 * With @p VFS_CFG_USE_MUTUAL_EXCLUSION enabled, the FatFS and LittleFS
 * drivers serialize the calls to their native library:
 * - FatFS uses one module mutex for all its volumes, node operations and
 *   mount and unmount, because FatFS keeps global state;
 * - LittleFS uses one mutex per driver instance for its native state, node
 *   operations and mount, unmount and format, instances on independent
 *   storage progress concurrently.
 * .
 * A leaf mutex is held across the native calls, the storage callbacks and the
 * I/O waits, so @p FF_FS_REENTRANT and the LittleFS thread-safe build are not
 * required. The LittleFS bindings still enable the thread-safe build by
 * default, the native locks then nest inside the driver mutex. Storage
 * callbacks must not reenter the VFS and direct native calls must not overlap
 * driver operations. Shared storage hardware still needs its own
 * synchronization, the LittleFS HAL binding provides it with its optional lock
 * hooks and the HAL exclusive access. The mutexes do not lift the native file
 * sharing restrictions and do not make multi-call sequences atomic.
 *
 * With the option disabled, a leaf file system is reentrant only as far as
 * its implementation is: FatFS requires @p FF_FS_REENTRANT, LittleFS its
 * thread-safe build with the lock callbacks.
 *
 * ROMFS data is immutable and needs no mutex. Stream drivers and ROMFS
 * dynamic callbacks follow the synchronization of their backend. ChibiFS is
 * not implemented and is not covered. Custom drivers and callbacks follow
 * the same rules, the option does not synchronize the shared backend state
 * they own.
 *
 * @subsection vfs_arch_descriptors Descriptors
 *
 * Each descriptor of the Newlib bindings owns a node reference. Table
 * insertion, lookup and detachment use short critical sections, read, write
 * and fstat keep their node referenced while running outside them. Close
 * detaches the descriptor before releasing the node, so its number can be
 * reused while an earlier operation still finishes on the original node.
 * Open rejects nodes overriding the reference methods with @c ENOTSUP and the
 * bindings require synchronized OOP reference counters, @p OOP_USE_NOTHING is
 * rejected. This protection does not depend on
 * @p VFS_CFG_USE_MUTUAL_EXCLUSION and does not serialize the operations on
 * the same descriptor.
 *
 * The descriptor table of a sandbox is owned by its host thread. The host
 * registers descriptors only while the sandbox is stopped. Close, cleanup and
 * @p dup2() detach a descriptor before releasing its node.
 *
 * @subsection vfs_arch_callers Callers
 *
 * - The default root, @p vfs_root, is set by the application before any user
 *   starts and stays valid while they run. The top-level path API uses its
 *   current directory, shared by all its users: a directory change followed
 *   by another call is not an atomic sequence, and shell commands running
 *   concurrently share it too.
 * - The ELF loader functions taking a file node borrow the caller reference
 *   and use the file position for the whole call. @p sbElfLoadFile() and the
 *   sandbox dynamic startup open and own their node instead.
 * - The HTTP bindings keep their file system pointer stable until all files
 *   are closed. Each HTTP file owns a node reference and is serialized by the
 *   HTTP server, close detaches the node from the file before releasing it.
 * - ROMFS descriptions and callback tables are immutable. Dynamic and
 *   compression callbacks synchronize their own shared state, can be called
 *   while a buffer pair is reserved and must not wait for another one,
 *   including through an allocating root operation. Native file system
 *   storage callbacks must not reenter the VFS.
 * .
 */

/*
 * VFS core class diagram, PlantUML source, not part of the documentation.
 *
 * @startuml {vfs_arch.png} "VFS Core"
 * abstract class vfs_fs_c {
 *   + stat(absolute_path)
 *   + open_directory(absolute_path)
 *   + open_file(absolute_path)
 *   + unlink(absolute_path)
 *   + rename(absolute_oldpath, absolute_newpath)
 *   + mkdir(absolute_path)
 *   + rmdir(absolute_path)
 * }
 * class vfs_overlay_driver_c {
 *   - overlaid_fs: vfs_fs_c *
 *   - mounted_fs[]: vfs_fs_c *
 * }
 * class vfs_root_c {
 *   - current_directory
 *   - path_prefix: const char *
 *   + change_current_directory(path)
 *   + get_current_directory()
 *   + open(path)
 * }
 * abstract class vfs_node_c {
 *   - fs: vfs_fs_c *
 * }
 * abstract class vfs_directory_node_c
 * abstract class vfs_file_node_c
 *
 * vfs_fs_c <|-- vfs_overlay_driver_c
 * vfs_overlay_driver_c <|-- vfs_root_c
 * vfs_node_c <|-- vfs_directory_node_c
 * vfs_node_c <|-- vfs_file_node_c
 * vfs_node_c "*" --> "1" vfs_fs_c : owner
 * vfs_overlay_driver_c "1" o-- "*" vfs_fs_c : mounts
 * @enduml
 */
